  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 110 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nSmoke from fires in Canada and the West Coast pivoted into our area \nthis morning behind a weak cold front. This smoke has mostly \ndispersed as of the early afternoon. Current satellite and radar \nshows clouds and some pockets of light rain across northern Larimer \nand Weld counties. Even behind the cold front, the hot stretch for \nNortheast Colorado will continue with another day of 90's forecast \nin Denver- making this 19 out of 21 days this month at or above 90 \nF. Highs today across the plains will be slightly \"cooler\" than \nyesterday, but still in the low to mid 90s. \n\nThe high pressure has elongated compared to yesterday, putting us \nmore on the periphery, and opening the door for scattered showers \nand thunderstorms. These will favor the northern foothills and \nmountains including the Cameron Peak and Alexander Mountain burn \nscars today. It is still uncertain whether or not the Urban Corridor \nand plains will receive any rainfall today, but any storms that form \nwould likely be associated with outflow boundaries from storms to \nthe north. The latest CAMs are not particularly excited about this \npotential.\n\nThe pattern shift towards monsoonal moisture will be in full effect \nby Wednesday and Thursday. The high pressure will shift off to the \nsouth over TX and NM and it is becoming increasingly likely that a \nshortwave will move through on Thursday. Low level southeast flow \nwill advect more moisture to the region, so dewpoints should \ngenerally remain in the 50s for much of the week. Dewpoints could \neven briefly reach the low 60s on Thursday as seen in recent model \nguidance.\n\nWe still expect PWATs to be 150-200% of normal for Wednesday with \neven a slight increase in PWATs on Thursday (approaching \nclimatological record high daily values). Storm motion could be slow \non Wednesday given weak flow aloft, but will increase on Thursday \nwith the arrival of the aforementioned shortwave. This should \nslightly increase rain coverage. Each afternoon, convection will \nstart in the high country and spread into the plains by late \nafternoon to early evening. Rainfall totals will generally be around \n0.25 to 0.75 inches with some isolated totals of 1.00 to perhaps \n2.00 inches by Thursday evening. The burn scars in our area will \nneed to be monitored, but rainfall rates should stay below flash \nflood guidance across the area. The only exception would be if we \nget training of storms. Given increased clouds and rainfall, high \ntemperatures will \"cool\" into the upper 80s to low 90s for both days.\n\nThe heat starts its return to the area on Friday and then increases \non Saturday and Sunday as the high pressure expands and shifts to be \ncentered near the Four Corners region. Scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will remain possible on Friday, but likely will stay \nconfined to the mountains. We have continued to nudge highs up into \nthe upper 90s to near 100 for both Saturday and Sunday, thus record \nhighs could be in play. A Heat Advisory could also be needed as we \nget closer to the weekend.\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to depict warm temperatures with highs \njust below 100 degrees for much of next week across the plains. The \nECMWF does try to shift the high pressure and bring some more rain \nto the area by mid-week, but confidence remains low on this solution \nat this time.",
